    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "can u explain exactly what the seller is responsible for under CIF Incoterms 2020? we're looking at a shipment of circuit boards from seoul to hamburg so need to know the risk transfer point and what insurance they gotta cover.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under CIF Incoterms 2020, the seller is responsible for delivering the goods to the carrier at the port of shipment (Seoul in your case), but here's the tricky part: they must pay for the main carriage and insurance to the port of destination (Hamburg), yet the risk transfer point is surprisingly early. The risk actually passes to the buyer once the goods are loaded on board the vessel at the port of shipment, meaning if your circuit boards fall off the ship in the middle of the Pacific, the buyer bears that loss, even though the seller paid for the ocean freight.\n\nAs for insurance, the seller must procure insurance coverage that meets the Institute Cargo Clauses (C) at minimum, which covers partial loss and total loss from accidents, but they aren't required to buy the more expensive \"All Risks\" coverage unless the contract specifies otherwise. It's a bit like hiring a security guard for your office who only protects against break-ins, not fire or theft, so you might want to check if your circuit boards need that extra layer of protection before the shipment leaves Seoul.\n\nJust remember, once the goods are safely on the ship in Seoul, the seller's job regarding the goods themselves is essentially done",
      "source": "target",
