    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so just to be clear, if the goods get damaged because of some unexpected thing during transit, even though the seller paid for the freight and insurance, im the one who has to file the claim? and does the seller have any obligation to monitor the shipment once it's actually on the water?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, absolutely, you are the one who has to file the claim if the circuit boards get damaged during transit, even though the seller paid for the freight and insurance. Under CIF, the risk of loss or damage transfers to you the moment the goods are loaded on board the vessel in Seoul. Once they are on that ship, they are effectively your responsibility, and any unexpected mishaps at sea—like a storm or a collision—are your burden to sort out.\n\nAs for the seller monitoring the shipment, they have no obligation to watch over the goods once they are on the water. Their job is strictly to get the goods to the port, hand them over to the carrier, and pay for the main carriage and insurance up to the destination port. They aren't responsible for the actual journey or the condition of the cargo once it's floating in the ocean. It's a bit like hiring a courier to deliver a package; once the courier drops it at the post office, the courier isn't liable if the package gets lost in the mail, even if they paid for the postage.\n\nHowever, since the seller *did* pay for the insurance, they technically have a duty to notify the insurer of any claims promptly, but that's a",
      "source": "target",
